WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.247 --> 00:00:02.510
- [Devin] Thanks to Mack
Weldon for keeping Legal Eagle

00:00:02.510 --> 00:00:04.965
in the air and helping me look fly.

00:00:04.965 --> 00:00:06.110
(eagle screeches)

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:07.940
- Imagine being a lawyer
skimming through old records

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:09.950
for months working on a
strategy to free your client

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:13.313
and this mo(beep) shows
up to court like this.

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:15.828
You can't fix stupid.

00:00:15.828 --> 00:00:20.130
(light, music-box-like music)

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:22.310
Hey, Legal Eagles, it's
time to think like a lawyer,

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:24.880
because I am outside of the Eagles' lair

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:28.050
in a secret location that
may or may not be related

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:30.560
to some skiing and snowboarding
that I am currently doing.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:32.970
And it's secret, not
because it's a true secret,

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:35.410
but maybe because I don't
want people to judge me

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:37.110
based on my skiing and
snowboarding skills.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:40.630
But because I am on location,
I asked you to provide me

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:43.150
with some great legal
memes, and once again,

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:44.520
you guys did not disappoint.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.410
I have so many legal memes to get through.

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:47.860
We're going to be laughing and crying

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:49.270
and everything in between.

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:52.140
So without further ado,
let's dig in once again

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:53.357
to Meme Review.

00:00:53.357 --> 00:00:55.540
(drums beat)

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:56.970
- Meme Review!

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:59.710
Meme Review trademark of
PewDiePie, all rights reserved.

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:01.480
All right, so let's
dig into our first meme

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:03.150
that you guys sent in.

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:04.830
The look you get when opposing counsel is

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:06.639
approaching the well without permission.

00:01:06.639 --> 00:01:07.510
(laughs)

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:10.130
So as you know, you are not
allowed to enter the well

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:10.963
without permission.

00:01:10.963 --> 00:01:12.860
The well is the distance
between counsel table

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:15.690
and the judge, and if you are
overly aggressive about it,

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:17.370
the bailiff will tackle you.

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:19.265
Don't do it. Get permission first.

00:01:19.265 --> 00:01:21.310
(drums beat)

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:24.890
- When the only case you can
find to cite is from 1823.

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:27.200
True story, this happens all the time.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:29.532
And yeah, we definitely feel like we are

00:01:29.532 --> 00:01:32.575
in Victorian England here
with the powdered wigs

00:01:32.575 --> 00:01:34.430
and the ridiculous ensembles.

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:37.055
The thing is the older
cases are easier facts,

00:01:37.055 --> 00:01:39.645
and the opinions that
judges write were way easier

00:01:39.645 --> 00:01:43.780
to understand a hundred or 200
years ago than they are now.

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:44.990
Now, if you get to court

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:46.520
and the appellate court writes a decision,

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:49.090
it's on a very, very marginal issue

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:51.590
that's going to be really
splitting some fine hairs.

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:55.310
So often the really, really
old cases are often the best.

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:57.801
And of course we lawyers
feel like we are characters

00:01:57.801 --> 00:01:59.114
in "Amadeus."

00:01:59.114 --> 00:02:01.270
(drums beat)

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:03.750
- Okay, when a first-year
redacts discovery.

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:05.570
A blurred face another blurred face,

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:08.994
and the woman whose
face was blurred before.

00:02:08.994 --> 00:02:11.676
First-year associates are the worst.

00:02:11.676 --> 00:02:15.490
When you graduate from law
school, you then often go

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:18.040
to work for a big firm,
and when that happens,

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:20.130
the thing that you realize is
law school didn't prepare you

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:24.140
for anything, so you are
really being an apprentice

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:25.970
for a law firm, and guess what?

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:26.810
You're going to make some mistakes

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:28.920
like redacting things that are obvious

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.710
and then forgetting to redact
the exact same thing later on.

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:33.490
So yeah, first-years:

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:35.257
definitely double-check your redactions.

00:02:35.257 --> 00:02:37.400
(drums beat)

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:38.880
- When your client tells you he's done

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:40.130
his own legal research.

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:44.320
Oh, this happened to me
a couple of days ago.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:47.820
It wasn't a client so much
as a potential opposing party

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:50.930
who had clearly done some
legal research on their own,

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:53.760
by going on Google which
is the equivalent of going

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:56.410
on WebMD and then diagnosing
yourself with cancer.

00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:58.500
Do not do your own legal research.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:02:59.610
You will be wrong.

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:01.800
You will look like an incredible idiot

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.840
to anyone that actually
knows what they're doing.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:05.994
Friends don't let friends
do their own legal research.

00:03:05.994 --> 00:03:08.120
(drums beat)

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:10.048
- When someone tells me
they got into law school.

00:03:10.048 --> 00:03:12.390
My deepest sympathies.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:15.210
Oh man, AttorneyProblems is
really knocking it out here.

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:16.473
Law school can be a lot of fun.

00:03:16.473 --> 00:03:19.230
Law school can also be
very, very terrible.

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:21.450
And like the video that I did last week

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:23.030
about "My Crazy Ex-Girlfriend,"

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:24.590
a lot of people really, really hate it

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:25.950
and they say don't be a lawyer.

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:27.730
I am not necessarily one of those people.

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:29.180
Some people should not go to law school

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:31.390
but some people definitely
should go to law school.

00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:33.640
So if you go to law school,
you have my sympathies

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:35.536
but there are ways to get through it.

00:03:35.536 --> 00:03:37.790
(drums beat)

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:40.620
- Um, yeah, if you could
stop calling every other day

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:42.552
for a case update,
that'd be great. (laughs)

00:03:42.552 --> 00:03:45.200
So I think this is about a client

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:46.450
that wants too many updates.

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:48.390
I don't really mind that.

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:50.250
The clients need to be
informed and frankly,

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:52.310
it's the duty of the lawyer
to reach out to their client

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:53.500
and give them updates.

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:57.330
So often lawyers don't
give updates nearly enough

00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:59.330
but yeah, they can they can definitely ask

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:00.399
for too many updates.

00:04:00.399 --> 00:04:02.660
(drums beat)

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:04.290
- Me as a lawyer: my client is innocent.

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:06.850
Them: your client is on video
dragging a lifeless body

00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:08.290
into the woods.

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:09.485
I said what I said.

00:04:09.485 --> 00:04:13.640
You know, it's not so much
video that is the thing

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:16.270
that really kills clients,
at least in a civil context.

00:04:16.270 --> 00:04:18.320
That definitely does happen
in a criminal context,

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:21.120
but in a civil context
which is most of what I do,

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:22.630
it's really the emails.

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:25.360
The emails will kill
you every single time.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:27.770
People don't understand
that those emails are going

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:30.120
to be used against you in a court of law.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:31.640
The lawyers are basically stuck

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:34.200
with arguing what we have to argue.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:35.150
I said what I said.

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:37.180
(drums beat)

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:39.330
- When the old dude asks if
you're the court reporter.

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:41.960
Ooh, Ooh, that's bad, yeah.

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:44.830
Law often can be an old boys club

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:48.283
and they will make all kinds
of really sexist remarks,

00:04:48.283 --> 00:04:52.240
and it's a problem for female
young associates especially,

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:55.085
that they get mistaken for
court reporters or staff

00:04:55.085 --> 00:04:56.850
or something else.

00:04:56.850 --> 00:04:59.792
Times will change eventually,
and people will understand

00:04:59.792 --> 00:05:03.231
that that's not appropriate
but it definitely happens.

00:05:03.231 --> 00:05:05.350
(drums beat)

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:07.620
- The sales contract had
a few strings attached.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:09.456
I played with them for two hours.

00:05:09.456 --> 00:05:11.734
(laughs) Aww, a kitty.

00:05:11.734 --> 00:05:12.567
(cat meows)

00:05:12.567 --> 00:05:14.250
- I don't think this needs
any explanation from me.

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:15.702
This is just a perfect joke.

00:05:15.702 --> 00:05:17.700
(drums beat)

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:19.870
- When you're a law student
that hasn't argued with anyone

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:20.890
for a few seconds.

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:22.910
I will argue with anyone about anything.

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:24.250
(laughs)

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:25.440
Okay, yeah.

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:28.120
Law students have a
well-deserved reputation

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:29.020
for arguing a lot.

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:30.850
In fact, there's actually a term for that.

00:05:30.850 --> 00:05:34.630
The person or people that
have to argue all the time,

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:36.720
they're called gunners and we hate them.

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:37.890
They just can't shut up.

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:39.690
They have to have the last word.

00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:40.810
They have to ask questions.

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:44.210
Yeah, they will argue
about anything with anyone.

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:45.070
They're terrible people.

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:45.961
Don't be a gunner.

00:05:45.961 --> 00:05:47.920
(drums beat)

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:49.370
- Civil law in a nutshell:

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:50.830
General rule, exception.

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:51.663
Oh my God.

00:05:51.663 --> 00:05:53.690
Oh, that really hits home.

00:05:53.690 --> 00:05:55.640
For example, we've talked
about hearsay a lot

00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:56.810
on this channel.

00:05:56.810 --> 00:05:59.950
As you probably know, there
are lots of hearsay exceptions.

00:05:59.950 --> 00:06:02.510
And as you probably know, the exceptions

00:06:02.510 --> 00:06:06.610
to the hearsay rule swallow
the hearsay rule in general.

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:09.840
Most things that are considered
hearsay will come in.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:11.970
Like this dam, the dam is broken

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:14.230
and all of that stuff is
coming in, and frankly,

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:16.030
I don't think that's the worst thing.

00:06:16.030 --> 00:06:17.890
Civil law, especially
civil procedure, man,

00:06:17.890 --> 00:06:22.025
the exceptions really preponderate
over the general rule.

00:06:22.025 --> 00:06:24.100
(drums beat)

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:25.740
- Courtroom exchange of the day.

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:27.360
Doctor, before you performed the autopsy

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:29.030
did you check for a pulse?
No.

00:06:29.030 --> 00:06:30.190
Did you check for blood pressure?

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:31.023
No.

00:06:31.023 --> 00:06:31.856
Did you check for breathing?

00:06:31.856 --> 00:06:32.689
No.

00:06:32.689 --> 00:06:34.510
So then is it possible
that the patient was alive

00:06:34.510 --> 00:06:35.660
when you began the autopsy?

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:36.493
No.

00:06:36.493 --> 00:06:37.870
How can you be sure, Doctor?

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:40.470
Because his brain was
sitting on my desk in a jar.

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:43.160
But could the patient have
still been alive, nevertheless?

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:44.940
Yes, it is possible that
he could have been alive

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:46.027
and practicing law.

00:06:46.027 --> 00:06:48.260
(laughs)

00:06:48.260 --> 00:06:50.248
I definitely have seen this one before.

00:06:50.248 --> 00:06:52.500
It's been floating around
the internet for a long time.

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:55.260
I would love for someone to track down

00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:58.060
to see if this is actually a
real deposition transcript.

00:06:58.060 --> 00:06:59.700
It's just too good to be true.

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:02.770
I really want it to be true.

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:04.150
I kind of doubt that it is.

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:07.190
And also, I don't think that
an autopsy would have begun

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:08.770
with a brain in a jar.

00:07:08.770 --> 00:07:11.080
I think you would have
to perform the autopsy

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:12.410
for their brain to be removed.

00:07:12.410 --> 00:07:14.540
If only there was some sort
of medical professional

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:17.910
that could let me know
how autopsies are done

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:19.555
that would be really
helpful in this situation.

00:07:19.555 --> 00:07:22.620
- Hey, Legal Eagle, Dr. Francis here.

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:25.570
Now I'm somewhat dubious
about this particular meme

00:07:25.570 --> 00:07:27.830
and I'd like to present
the following evidence

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:29.460
for your consideration.

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:32.402
You see, an autopsy or
post-mortem normally begins

00:07:32.402 --> 00:07:35.680
after an external examination of the body

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:37.941
by making a large incision
from the neck all the way

00:07:37.941 --> 00:07:40.580
down to the pelvic bone.

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:43.210
Sometimes it's Y-shaped,
starting at either shoulder

00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:44.810
and meeting around the xiphoid sternum

00:07:44.810 --> 00:07:47.200
and again, continuing down to the pelvis.

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:51.130
At that point, all the
internal organs of the chest

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:53.800
and the abdomen are removed and inspected.

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:56.860
Only, at that point is
the skull cavity opened

00:07:56.860 --> 00:07:58.140
and the brain removed.

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:00.940
So I find it hard to believe
that the brain would have been

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:04.560
on the pathologist's desk at
the start of the procedure.

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:08.437
Hence, I submit to you thus
that this joke is guilty

00:08:08.437 --> 00:08:11.470
of medical meme malpractice.

00:08:11.470 --> 00:08:12.450
Thanks, Rohin.

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:13.827
I learned something.

00:08:13.827 --> 00:08:15.900
(drums beat)

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:17.480
- I think I found my new lawyer.

00:08:17.480 --> 00:08:19.716
Just because you did it
doesn't mean you're guilty.

00:08:19.716 --> 00:08:20.950
(laughs)

00:08:20.950 --> 00:08:22.610
That's actually true, believe it or not.

00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:23.443
So for example,

00:08:23.443 --> 00:08:26.880
let's say that a person
kills another person.

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:28.720
You would think that that would be murder

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:30.350
but let's say that they
acted in self-defense.

00:08:30.350 --> 00:08:32.820
Just because you killed
someone, you did it,

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:34.580
doesn't necessarily
mean that you're guilty

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:36.340
of the crime of murder.

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:38.717
So yeah, this lawyer, he knows what's up.

00:08:38.717 --> 00:08:40.750
(drums beat)

00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:42.840
- 0L: people have to be exaggerating

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:44.100
about how miserable law school is.

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:45.950
There's just no way
it's as bad as they say.

00:08:45.950 --> 00:08:49.070
1L: I too was hustled,
scammed, bamboozled,

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:50.504
hoodwinked, led astray. (laughs)

00:08:50.504 --> 00:08:53.600
I wonder if that is in
reference to the Fyre Festival

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:55.190
that Ja Rule did.

00:08:55.190 --> 00:08:56.590
- [Man] The cheese sandwich.

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:01.490
That was when I knew this was done.

00:09:01.490 --> 00:09:04.090
- It's pretty much as
bad as they say it is.

00:09:04.090 --> 00:09:05.550
Doesn't mean that you can't have fun.

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:08.570
You definitely will have
fun, but man, 1L's the devil.

00:09:08.570 --> 00:09:11.880
Oh yeah, and 0Ls are people
that are accepted to law school,

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:13.630
but haven't yet started law school.

00:09:13.630 --> 00:09:16.000
So yeah, they definitely have
questions about law school

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:18.690
and it definitely sounds bad.

00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:19.680
And then you get to law school

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:22.526
and you find out it's pretty freaking bad.

00:09:22.526 --> 00:09:23.820
(chuckles)

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:28.548
- [Girl] Turn around, turn
this bus right around.

00:09:28.548 --> 00:09:30.720
(drums beat)

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:32.000
- Sprinkling your law school essay

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:33.320
with Latin legal terminologies

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:38.290
like non est factum, contra
preferentum, ratio descendis,

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:41.520
descend (chuckles), I
can't even pronounce that.

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:46.520
Ratio dicendendi. That's
a new, new one by me.

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:49.820
Onus of proof, obiter
dicta, stare decisis, okay.

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:51.580
Common misconception about lawyers:

00:09:51.580 --> 00:09:53.744
good lawyers really try to avoid adding

00:09:53.744 --> 00:09:56.250
too much Latin terminology

00:09:56.250 --> 00:09:59.850
and really any kind of Inside
Baseball legal terminologies.

00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:01.800
It's not persuasive.

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:03.120
Sometimes you have to do it.

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:05.290
But good legal writers
will do everything they can

00:10:05.290 --> 00:10:09.076
to avoid adding the kind of
language that isn't going

00:10:09.076 --> 00:10:10.850
to resonate with most people.

00:10:10.850 --> 00:10:14.420
So, yeah, law students
like to add that (beep)

00:10:14.420 --> 00:10:17.882
but good lawyers should generally
not if they can avoid it.

00:10:17.882 --> 00:10:19.940
(drums beat)

00:10:19.940 --> 00:10:20.930
- I am a law student.

00:10:20.930 --> 00:10:23.280
I have this weird self-esteem
issue where I hate myself

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:26.300
yet I still think I am
better than everyone else.

